    "word": "liuqiu",
    "lang_code": "en",
    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "dea8a4f2-3f97-334d-8a18-5ab148280860",
                "word": "Liuqiu",
                "pos": "name",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "120accfb-5567-396b-9aa9-1fd8cd5a379b",
                        "entry_id": "dea8a4f2-3f97-334d-8a18-5ab148280860",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Synonym of Ryukyu (“island chain”): the Mandarin Chinese-derived name."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "e5417d12-0f54-4751-bcbd-68e7e5456d68",
                                "sense_id": "120accfb-5567-396b-9aa9-1fd8cd5a379b",
                                "text": "There is moreover in the woods marvelous ſtore of Braſil and Ebony, wherewith an hundred Juncks are every year laden to be tranſported to China, Hainan, the Lequios, Camboya and Camphaa ;"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "da618b40-b2aa-4488-9121-d1806e7c60a0",
                                "sense_id": "120accfb-5567-396b-9aa9-1fd8cd5a379b",
                                "text": "Japan, emerging from its century-long seclusion, annexed the Liuqiu (Ryukus) Islands and, by defeating the Chinese in Korea (1894-95), began to exert control over that peninsula as well as the island of Taiwan."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "63e98bfc-8178-4ecc-a9ec-aa0b3b22630a",
                                "sense_id": "120accfb-5567-396b-9aa9-1fd8cd5a379b",
                                "text": "Relations between the Qing court and the king of the Liuqiu Islands were maintained regularly; as the Qing emperors welcomed ambassadors and tribute missions from the Liuqius, Chinese coastal trade with the Liuqiu Islands flourished."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "12e783b8-6bae-38bb-8b54-f69769665140",
                        "entry_id": "dea8a4f2-3f97-334d-8a18-5ab148280860",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A rural township of Pingtung County, Taiwan, encompassing Liuqiu Island."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "a2b10c2c-0072-4c6e-8c6e-192bb327515f",
                                "sense_id": "12e783b8-6bae-38bb-8b54-f69769665140",
                                "text": "With the coming of the bluefin tuna fishing season, a Liuqiu Township-based fishing boat caught the county's first tuna fish of this year, CNA reported."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f140c6a1-c210-3d19-8931-81c9d57d7e89",
                        "entry_id": "dea8a4f2-3f97-334d-8a18-5ab148280860",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The island of Liuqiu, Pingtung County, Taiwan."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "62e77f90-7c66-40cc-818d-f293a677dc5c",
                                "sense_id": "f140c6a1-c210-3d19-8931-81c9d57d7e89",
                                "text": "Much of the ocean they surveyed last summer was above 30 degrees Celsius for three months. The worst area was in Little Liuqiu, a coral island off the southwest coast in the Taiwan Strait where 55 percent of corals have now been seriously bleached."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0e5ff98f-3d69-4a3c-9238-fb6ae4fcf9b5",
                                "sense_id": "f140c6a1-c210-3d19-8931-81c9d57d7e89",
                                "text": "“Nothing is going to change after the military exercises, there’ll be one like this and then another,” said Li Wen-te, a 63-year-old retired fisherman in Liuqiu, an island off the southwestern coast of Taiwan, less than six miles from China’s drills.[…]\n“I cherish our freedom of speech and don’t want to be unified by China,” said Huang Chiu-hong, 47, the owner of a shop that sells fried sticks of braided dough, a local snack, on Liuqiu, the Taiwanese island."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "343d37aa-0c94-424b-aeda-1374024b6f7d",
                                "sense_id": "f140c6a1-c210-3d19-8931-81c9d57d7e89",
                                "text": "China said this week it would conduct more drills focusing on anti-submarine and sea assault operations - confirming the fears of some security analysts and diplomats that it would keep up the pressure on Taiwan's defences. read more\nBut on Liuqiu island, a picturesque tourist spot near one of the areas where the Chinese military was conducting exercises last week, people were largely indifferent."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
